"""Days1–2: define a small product, then build its backend deliberately."""
L(0,'1. Start with a learner problem, not a pile of features','Turn a real need into a small full-stack product you can demonstrate.',
 [('Full-stack vertical slice','A usable journey crossing browser, HTTP, server rules and storage. One working create-and-retrieve flow is stronger evidence than four disconnected layers.'),('User story','A short statement of who needs an action and why. Acceptance criteria supply the observable evidence; the story alone is not a test.')],
 'Our learner captures something, explains it in their own words and returns later. Study Notes supports that small loop. It is a separate practice app, not a replacement for this learning platform. The browser supplies a convenient workspace; Python owns the rules and persistence. We extend the known Notes API so attention can shift to integration and delivery instead of changing every tool.\n\nOpen spec.json before touching the UI. Read S1 through S6 and describe one person completing each journey. Avoid vague goals such as “make a professional app”. A reviewer should be able to create a fictional account, save a note, refresh, sign in and retrieve it. That is a visible outcome with a clear boundary.',
 'import json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nspec=json.loads(Path("spec.json").read_text())\nprint(spec["name"])\nprint(spec["goal"])\nprint("Stories:",len(spec["stories"]))',
 'Extract the story identifiers from the supplied specification.',
 'import json\nfrom pathlib import Path\ns=json.loads(Path("spec.json").read_text())\nprint([story["id"] for story in s["stories"]])',
 'Write a tiny vertical slice rather than an unrelated list of technical layers.',
 'journey=["write a thought","submit owned note","commit to database","retrieve it after sign-in"]\nprint(" -> ".join(journey))',
 [('What proves a vertical slice?','A real journey through all required layers','A beautiful empty page','An unconnected database','Follow one user outcome end to end.'),('Does Study Notes replace Study Hub progress?','No','Yes','Only after registration','The teaching project uses independent storage.')],
 'Build every possible screen before deciding the learner outcome.','Choose one small journey and prove it through the actual system.','A clear problem keeps both the design and the tests focused.',syntax='learner need -> story -> acceptance criteria -> vertical slice -> evidence')

L(0,'2. Make acceptance criteria observable','Describe success and rejection before implementation.',
 [('Acceptance criteria; Given / When / Then','A concrete starting condition, action and observable result. Include persisted data and denied actions, not just an animation.'),('Negative acceptance case','A requirement about what the system must reject or preserve when a request is invalid or belongs to somebody else.')],
 '“Notes work” is too broad. Given Asha has signed in, when she submits a valid title and body, then the API returns201 and a later authenticated read returns the same saved content. Given Ben signs in, reading Asha’s note returns404. The second case protects the central product promise, not an optional polish detail.\n\nKeep UI and HTTP evidence distinct. A Python TestClient can prove the response contract without opening Chrome; it cannot prove the user can find the Save button. A browser check can prove that workflow but still needs targeted backend tests for boundary values. Decide what evidence each requirement needs, then use that distinction throughout the sprint.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    response=c.get("/v1/notes")\n    print("Anonymous status:",response.status_code)\n    print("Bearer challenge:",response.headers["www-authenticate"])',
 'Check that a valid signed-in note creates an observable resource.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.post("/v1/notes",headers=h,json={"title":"One outcome","body":"Explain it"})\n    print("Created:",r.status_code)\n    print("Read back:",c.get(r.headers["Location"],headers=h).json()["title"])',
 'Write one positive and one denied-case checklist item.',
 'checks={"owner can read saved note":True,"anonymous access rejected":True}\nprint("Required checks:",len(checks))\nprint("All observed:",all(checks.values()))',
 [('Which is testable acceptance?','A different account receives404 for my note','The app feels complete','Use a modern framework','An observable outcome supplies a clear verdict.'),('Can TestClient alone verify mouse and keyboard usability?','No','Always','Only with JSON','Real browser evidence covers different behaviour.')],
 'Accept the story because the submit button changes colour.','Verify server response, persisted result and the relevant rejection path.','Acceptance criteria connect the user promise to evidence.')

L(0,'3. Cut scope without cutting the learning','Separate the essential product from valuable future work.',
 [('Scope boundary','A deliberate statement of what this release includes and excludes. Deferring a feature is different from quietly implying it exists.'),('Non-goal','A named capability outside the current release, such as sharing notes or recovering forgotten passwords. State the consequence for users.')],
 'This sprint includes private plain-text notes, known tags, completion, literal title search, pages and session logout. It excludes uploads, rich text, collaboration, password recovery and offline writes. Each exclusion reduces an entire family of storage, permissions, security and UX decisions. Keep the excluded list visible while building.\n\nDepth means understanding the chosen slice completely. It does not require adding every idea this week. For example, memory-only tokens make refresh require sign-in, and the product explicitly explains that behaviour. Simultaneous edits currently use last successful write wins; no conflict detection is claimed. Those are honest design choices to improve later after the core product has reproducible evidence.',
 'import json\nfrom pathlib import Path\ns=json.loads(Path("spec.json").read_text())\nprint("Shared notes deferred:","shared notes" in s["out_of_scope"])\nprint("Write policy:",s["write_policy"])',
 'Separate a small release scope from future candidates.',
 'must={"owned CRUD","search","sign out"};later={"uploads","shared editing"}\nprint("Current features:",sorted(must))\nprint("No overlap:",must.isdisjoint(later))',
 'Find the documented offline-write boundary rather than assuming a queue exists.',
 'import json\nfrom pathlib import Path\ns=json.loads(Path("spec.json").read_text())\nprint("Offline write queue deferred:","offline write queue" in s["out_of_scope"])',
 [('Does a smaller scope require shallow explanations?','No','Yes','Only for frontend','Study the selected workflow deeply and name deferred work.'),('Is a deferred password-recovery flow already available?','No','Yes','Only for existing accounts','Document the actual product boundary.')],
 'Advertise collaboration and offline writes because they are future ideas.','Name the implemented scope and the concrete missing behaviours.','A trustworthy small release is easier to learn from and review.')

L(0,'4. Model ownership and relationships before screens','Explain why identity, notes, tags and sessions have separate storage roles.',
 [('Entity and invariant','An entity has identity and stored attributes. An invariant is a rule that must remain true, such as every note referencing a real owner.'),('Association table','note_tags connects many notes to many shared tag entries. Deleting a note removes its links, not the shared tag catalog.')],
 'The supplied schema has users, notes, tags, note_tags and token_sessions. A note points to its owner using a foreign key; the signed-in identity supplies that owner. Tag names form a tiny shared catalog, while the selected links belong to individual notes. Login-session rows allow one bearer session to be revoked independently.\n\nSketch these arrows before designing forms. The editor may request title, body and completed, but it must not accept an arbitrary owner_id. A username label in the header does not establish identity. Authentication establishes the principal; scoped queries enforce ownership. Database constraints preserve structure underneath both correct and mistaken application code.',
 'from orm_api.models import Base,Note\nprint("Entities:",sorted(Base.metadata.tables))\nprint("Owner foreign key:",sorted(str(f.column) for f in Note.__table__.c.owner_id.foreign_keys))',
 'Identify the two columns that define a note-to-tag link.',
 'from orm_api.models import note_tags\nprint("Link identity:",[c.name for c in note_tags.primary_key.columns])',
 'Check whether an ownership field is writable through the create contract.',
 'from orm_api.schemas import NoteCreate\nprint("Writable fields:",list(NoteCreate.model_fields))\nprint("Client chooses owner:","owner_id" in NoteCreate.model_fields)',
 [('Who supplies the owner for a new note?','The authenticated server principal','A hidden browser input','The public URL alone','Identity must come from verified authentication.'),('Should deleting one note delete the shared python tag?','No','Always','Only on logout','Delete the association, not the shared catalog.')],
 'Trust a hidden owner_id field because users cannot normally see it.','Reject unexpected owner fields and derive ownership on the server.','Browser inputs are requests; server invariants are the authority.')

L(0,'5. Map screens to an explicit HTTP contract','Connect a UI action to method, path, payload and expected result.',
 [('API surface','The public methods, paths, schemas, authentication and statuses a client can rely on.'),('Screen-to-contract map','A description of which server operations support each visible workflow. One button can involve more than one transaction.')],
 'The workspace is not a second database implementation. It sends requests to the existing account and note API. Sign-in submits form data; note writes submit JSON; collection reads encode query parameters. A successful delete returns204 with no JSON body. These differences matter when writing one request helper.\n\nDraw the path for Save: POST or PATCH a note, then PUT its selected tags, then refresh the current collection. The two writes are separate transactions, so one can succeed while the other fails. Naming that boundary now avoids claiming atomic behaviour later. Use generated OpenAPI to confirm the route and input contracts rather than guessing from a button label.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    schema=c.get("/openapi.json").json()\n    print("Create:","post" in schema["paths"]["/v1/notes"])\n    print("Completion query:",[p["name"] for p in schema["paths"]["/v1/notes"]["get"]["parameters"]])',
 'Verify the successful delete contract on a fresh owned resource.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.post("/v1/notes",headers=h,json={"title":"Disposable","body":"Owned fixture"})\n    deleted=c.delete(r.headers["Location"],headers=h)\n    print("Status:",deleted.status_code)\n    print("Body bytes:",len(deleted.content))',
 'Write the transport choice for the login and note workflows.',
 'transport={"login":"form encoded","note write":"JSON","list filter":"URL query"}\nfor action,kind in transport.items():print(action+":",kind)',
 [('Does204 promise a JSON response body?','No','Always','Only for DELETE','Handle the empty successful response explicitly.'),('Is saving a note and its tags currently one transaction?','No','Yes','Only from the dashboard','The client must handle partial success honestly.')],
 'Send every request in the same format and parse every success as JSON.','Follow each endpoint’s input and response contract.','The browser integrates existing promises rather than inventing new ones.')

L(0,'6. Plan the sprint as evidence-producing slices','Sequence work and define completion without relying on a calendar.',
 [('Definition of done','A checklist of required evidence, not a feeling or a percentage. Include permission failures, browser usability, documentation and deployment boundaries.'),('Traceability','A connection from a user story to implementation and to a check that proves its acceptance criteria.')],
 'Build the backend contract first, then connect one browser journey and expand it. Keep a small task list: implement, verify, record. A blocker discovered in a denied or interrupted flow is real product work, not a distraction from polishing the happy path. Split the day into parts whenever understanding needs more time.\n\nFor this project, code checks, real browser checks and actual container checks are separate evidence. A public HTTPS flow from another device is another stage. Publishing source to GitHub does not execute Python on GitHub Pages. Keep an unfinished external verification pending in the release record instead of marking the whole milestone complete because a local screenshot looks good.',
 'import json\nfrom pathlib import Path\ns=json.loads(Path("spec.json").read_text())\nfor check in s["definition_of_done"]:print(check)',
 'Map a story to more than a file name.',
 'trace={"story":"S2","implementation":"owned note routes + dashboard","evidence":["CRUD test","two-owner denial","browser save flow"]}\nprint(trace["story"],"checks:",len(trace["evidence"]))',
 'Keep an unperformed deployment check visibly pending.',
 'evidence={"local tests":"passed","public phone flow":"pending"}\nprint("Fully released:",all(v=="passed" for v in evidence.values()))',
 [('Does GitHub Pages publication run the Python API?','No','Yes','Only with a Dockerfile','Static publication and an API runtime are separate.'),('What should an unperformed check say?','Pending','Passed','Probably fine','The record should describe observed evidence.')],
 'Move every task to done when the calendar day ends.','Finish the learning and record actual verification at each boundary.','A sprint is a delivery method, not permission to skip understanding.')

L(1,'1. Establish a project boundary and an application factory','Run a fresh app without borrowing older runtime data.',
 [('Composition root','The point that connects configuration, database/session lifetime, routes and middleware. create_app constructs an independent application.'),('Test fixture boundary','A helper owns the temporary app, logs, accounts and files for one check. It must not read a learner’s actual instance.')],
 'Work in docs/python-week43. Its public source continues the familiar API, but its w43 migration lineage and runtime store are independent. The application factory lets tests provide a temporary instance path while normal startup reads validated settings. This makes the same routes usable in different controlled environments.\n\nFollow startup deliberately: choose settings, construct the app, enter its lifespan, initialise or verify the schema and signing material, then serve requests. A Python import alone does not prove the server is ready. The provided practice context enters the real TestClient lifespan and cleans its owned temporary folder afterward. Never solve a test failure by pointing it at an existing personal database.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    print("App:",app.title)\n    print("Live:",c.get("/health").status_code)\n    print("Ready:",c.get("/health/ready").status_code)',
 'Inspect the independent schema head without opening any stored user data.',
 'from orm_api.database import HEAD\nprint("Week43 head:",HEAD)',
 'Confirm the dashboard and API explorer have different entry pages.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    print("Dashboard:","Study Notes" in c.get("/").text)\n    print("Explorer:","Local Notes API" in c.get("/docs").text)',
 [('What makes repeated app tests independent?','Owned temporary configuration and storage','Using the same personal database','Changing only the page title','Isolation belongs in the fixture and application lifecycle.'),('Does importing create_app itself perform every startup check?','No','Always','Only with SQLite','Startup/lifespan and import are distinct.')],
 'Copy a previous week’s instance directory to get a quick test login.','Create a fresh project store and disposable fixtures.','Known initial conditions make failures reproducible.',shell='python -m pip install -r requirements-dev.txt\npython -m pytest -q\npython app.py\n# Open http://127.0.0.1:8002/')

L(1,'2. Treat the schema and migrations as reviewed code','Explain fresh creation, expected revision and preservation on mismatch.',
 [('Migration lineage','An ordered history of schema changes. Week43 uses w43_001 through w43_003 and does not pretend another week’s database has that history.'),('Schema readiness gate','The API verifies a recognised schema and expected head before serving useful work. Unknown history is preserved for investigation.')],
 'Models describe the application mapping, while reviewed Alembic revisions describe how the database reaches it. This project creates a fresh local schema when it owns a new empty file. Known older files require an explicit controlled upgrade; unfamiliar tables/history are rejected. PostgreSQL startup uses a separate migration job before runtime workers begin.\n\nTrace the required tables and constraints. Foreign keys prevent orphaned owners and tag links; title/body checks complement request validation; indexes serve common owner-scoped access. Do not call create_all on every production request or reset a mismatched database to make a demo green. A migration is an operational change with compatibility and recovery consequences.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nfrom orm_api.database import revision\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    print("Database revision:",revision(app.state.engine))',
 'Read the note check-constraint names from the actual mapping.',
 'from orm_api.models import Note\nfrom sqlalchemy import CheckConstraint\nprint(sorted(c.name for c in Note.__table__.constraints if isinstance(c,CheckConstraint)))',
 'Confirm which revisions belong to this project.',
 'from orm_api.database import REVISIONS\nprint(sorted(REVISIONS))',
 [('Should an unfamiliar database be deleted automatically?','No','Always','Only in development','Preserve unknown state and review a migration.'),('Does an ORM model replace migration history?','No','Yes','Only on PostgreSQL','Mapping and controlled schema evolution serve different jobs.')],
 'Reset the database whenever the expected schema is missing.','Recognise the current state and apply reviewed changes with recovery evidence.','Preserving data is part of backend correctness.')

L(1,'3. Create an owned note inside an explicit transaction','Follow validated input through identity, flush, commit and response.',
 [('Flush versus commit','Flush sends pending SQL and obtains generated values inside the transaction. Commit makes the transaction durable; a flush alone is not a completed save.'),('Server-owned field','A field such as owner_id or created_at is assigned by server rules, rather than copied from untrusted JSON.')],
 'The create route validates title, body and completed, derives the owner from current_user, assigns a timestamp and adds the ORM row. Flush supplies the generated id for the result and Location header; the atomic context commits before the route returns success. If database work fails, the context rolls back.\n\nRead this as one contract, not isolated keywords. A201 response should mean the operation has passed the save boundary. A malicious owner_id in the JSON is rejected instead of silently changing ownership. The response exposes only the public note fields; password hashes and internal owner assignments are absent. Test both the persisted result and the rejected extra field.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.post("/v1/notes",headers=h,json={"title":"  A thought  ","body":"  Explain why  "})\n    print("Created:",r.status_code)\n    print("Clean title:",r.json()["title"])\n    print("Retrieved:",c.get(r.headers["Location"],headers=h).json()["body"])',
 'Attempt to choose another owner and check the status.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.post("/v1/notes",headers=h,json={"title":"No transfer","body":"Fixture","owner_id":2})\n    print("Rejected:",r.status_code)',
 'Show that the response is a public contract rather than a raw ORM dump.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.post("/v1/notes",headers=h,json={"title":"Public shape","body":"Fixture"}).json()\n    print("Owner exposed:","owner_id" in r)\n    print("Password exposed:","password_hash" in r)',
 [('Does flush guarantee the transaction is committed?','No','Always','Only for inserts','Commit is a separate transaction boundary.'),('Should create copy owner_id from JSON?','No','Yes','Only when hidden','The authenticated principal determines ownership.')],
 'Return success after adding an object without confirming the commit.','Complete the transaction before returning a successful save contract.','The caller relies on a meaningful success boundary.')

L(1,'4. Search, filter and paginate one owned collection','Combine predicates before counting and selecting page rows.',
 [('Filter composition','Owner, literal search and optional completed predicates must apply to both the count and the selected rows.'),('Stable pagination','A deterministic order plus bounded limit/offset. Reset the offset when changing filters and recover when deletion removes the last page.')],
 'Start every collection query with the signed-in owner. Add a literal case-lowered title search and, only when completed is not None, an explicit boolean predicate. False is a real filter value; testing if completed would accidentally ignore it. Count the filtered collection before applying page offset and limit.\n\nThe endpoint orders by note id to make ordinary pagination predictable. It bounds requests, but offset pagination is not a snapshot while other clients insert or delete. SQLAlchemy contains with autoescape treats % and _ as characters rather than wildcard commands. Search uses each database’s lower/collation rules, so this is not a promise of every language’s full-text search semantics.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.get("/v1/notes",headers=h,params={"q":"%","completed":"false","limit":1})\n    print("Total:",r.json()["total"])\n    print("Title:",r.json()["items"][0]["title"])',
 'Prove that false and true select different collections.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    for value in ["false","true"]:\n        r=c.get("/v1/notes",headers=h,params={"completed":value})\n        print(value,r.json()["total"])',
 'Read the second one-item page and retain the filtered total.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    p=c.get("/v1/notes?limit=1&offset=1",headers=h).json()\n    print("Total:",p["total"])\n    print("Page rows:",len(p["items"]))\n    print("Title:",p["items"][0]["title"])',
 [('How should an optional boolean filter distinguish omitted from false?','Compare with None','Use if completed','Convert false to missing','False is a valid requested value.'),('Should total count include notes from another owner?','No','Yes','Only when search is empty','Every collection operation stays owner-scoped.')],
 'Use if completed and count all users before filtering page rows.','Test completed is not None and reuse the same owned predicates.','Count and page contents must describe the same collection.',syntax='predicate = (Note.owner_id == user.id, search_predicate)\nif completed is not None:\n    predicate += (Note.completed == completed,)')

L(1,'5. Update and delete without losing false or ownership','Distinguish partial changes, replacement and confirmed deletion.',
 [('PATCH contract','Only explicitly supplied writable fields change. exclude_unset distinguishes an omitted field from a supplied false value.'),('Deletion boundary','A confirmed204 means the owned note was deleted. The UI must not remove a row merely because the user clicked a button.')],
 'An update begins by retrieving the owned note. The patch model rejects empty objects, explicit nulls, unknown fields and wrong boolean types. It then applies supplied fields inside a transaction. A complete replacement has its own required fields. These rules prevent a convenient frontend from becoming the only data validator.\n\nDelete also scopes the row to its owner and commits before returning204. Afterward, an authenticated read returns404. A second client may have removed the note first, so the UI needs a useful unavailable-state response. Concurrent editing currently uses last successful write wins; proper version checks or conditional requests are a separate future improvement.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    url="/v1/notes/1"\n    c.patch(url,headers=h,json={"completed":True})\n    r=c.patch(url,headers=h,json={"completed":False})\n    print("False retained:",r.json()["completed"] is False)\n    print("Unchanged title:",r.json()["title"])',
 'Verify an invalid patch preserves the stored note.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    before=c.get("/v1/notes/1",headers=h).json()\n    print("Empty patch:",c.patch("/v1/notes/1",headers=h,json={}).status_code)\n    print("Preserved:",c.get("/v1/notes/1",headers=h).json()==before)',
 'Check the result after a successful owned deletion.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    print("Delete:",c.delete("/v1/notes/1",headers=h).status_code)\n    print("Later read:",c.get("/v1/notes/1",headers=h).status_code)',
 [('Should a supplied false be discarded as missing?','No','Always','Only for PATCH','Omission and false are different inputs.'),('Does the current project detect simultaneous edit conflicts?','No','Yes','Only from the same browser','Document last-write-wins until version checking is implemented.')],
 'Remove a row immediately and assume a failed delete probably worked.','Wait for server confirmation and handle missing or failed results explicitly.','The UI should represent the known saved state.')

L(1,'6. Complete the tag relationship contract','Keep shared catalog values separate from owned note links.',
 [('Catalog validation','The project accepts only unique names from python, api and database. Known names are deliberately a small scope choice.'),('Partial success across requests','Saving a note and then its tags can leave a saved note with unchanged tags if the second request fails. A browser retry needs the saved note id.')],
 'The tag update checks ownership, resolves requested catalog rows and replaces the note’s links in one database transaction. Duplicate or unknown names are invalid. An empty list intentionally clears the links. Deleting a note cascades its association links while leaving the shared tag catalog available.\n\nDo not confuse that single tag transaction with the full browser Save workflow. The note write and tag write are separate calls. The dashboard changes its editor state to the returned note id immediately after the first succeeds; if tags fail it retries an update, not another create. We will test that interrupted path in a real browser rather than claiming two requests are atomic.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    r=c.put("/v1/notes/1/tags",headers=h,json={"names":["python","api"]})\n    print("Saved tags:",r.json())\n    print("Read tags:",c.get("/v1/notes/1/tags",headers=h).json())',
 'Check duplicates and unknown names without changing the catalog.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    for names in [["api","api"],["missing"]]:\n        print(c.put("/v1/notes/1/tags",headers=h,json={"names":names}).status_code)',
 'Clear one note’s links and prove the catalog remains.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice() as (app,c,h):\n    print("Cleared:",c.put("/v1/notes/1/tags",headers=h,json={"names":[]}).json())\n    print("Catalog:",c.get("/v1/tags",headers=h).json())',
 [('What should a retry retain after the note saved but tags failed?','The saved note id','Only an empty form','A second create command','Retry the existing resource rather than duplicating it.'),('Does clearing selected tags delete the shared catalog?','No','Yes','Only when empty','Links and catalog entities are different.')],
 'Repeat POST after every tag error because the overall Save did not finish.','Retain the confirmed resource id and retry its remaining update.','Partial success needs an explicit recovery path.')
